  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1021 PM MST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nA slow moving trough with a closed low centered over the Great\nBasin will drift across New Mexico Saturday afternoon and evening,\nbringing somewhat breezy west to southwest winds with it Friday\nand Saturday. A moderately strong Pacific cold front will push \nthrough late Saturday. As a result, above normal high temperatures\nFriday (lower to mid-70s in the lowlands) will drop to a few\ndegrees below normal Saturday (lower to middle-60s in the\nlowlands). \n\nDiminishing winds Saturday night, and a cooler/drier air mass,\nwill result in stronger radiational cooling. Min temperatures by\nSunday morning may rival the coldest of the season so far.\nTemperatures in the usual cold spots of the Gila region will flirt\nwith the upper-10s, and freezing temperatures are likely in the\nHatch and Mesilla Valleys, along with most of the Tularosa Basin,\nand several of the typical cooler low-lying spots in the southern\nNM deserts. If the winds manage to stay up a bit, then\ntemperatures will be a little milder, but we'll have another shot\nat expanded freezing temperatures Sunday night/Monday morning.\nThe auto-populated NBM grids did not really show the freezing\ntemperatures in many of these areas, and were manually edited\nbased on bias corrected data and experience. \n\nAnother, faster-moving upper level trough will sweep through the\narea Monday night into Tuesday, with a slight chance of light rain\nshowers, but overall the quieter pattern (more typical of\nNovember) looks to continue into next week.",
